        "title": "World Cup sticker shock: The ugly cost of the beautiful game's grand event",
        "description": "On FIFA's resale marketplace, in which soccer's organizing body takes an additional cut from transaction fees, prices have soared into four to six figures for consumers. There have even been [tickets listed for more than $2 million](https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/48572372/world-cup-final-tickets-listed-fifa-resale-2-million).\n\nLawmakers across the U.S. have questioned FIFA's perplexing multi-category ticket-selling process, leading to [a subpoena from the attorneys general of New York and New Jersey](https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/48885837/new-jersey-new-york-subpoena-20926-fifa-world-cup-tickets), who stated in a joint announcement that tickets have \"far exceeded the prices for any previous World Cup tournament\" thanks in part to dynamic pricing that can wildly inflate costs.\n\nIn the last World Cup, [prices were set between $70 to $1,600](https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/47325927/fifa-2026-world-cup-ticket-prices-supporter-tier) when tickets were revealed.\n\nThe proposed face value of tickets ranged between $21 to $323 (not including suites) for group-stage games. The final, again excluding suites, ranged from $128 to $1,550. To quote the bid proposal, \"Prices have been projected based on those from historic FIFA World Cups and validated against comparable ticket prices within the host countries.\"\n\nFIFA confirmed to ESPN that the cheapest tickets originally went on sale at $60, making that revenue forecast appear extremely conservative.\n\nHowever, the number of $60 tickets for each game was likely to be in the hundreds, rather than thousands, according to [The Associated Press](https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/47325927/fifa-2026-world-cup-ticket-prices-supporter-tier).\n\nFor example, in Los Angeles, where the [U.S. men's national team](https://www.espn.com/soccer/team?id=660) will play twice, the average cheapest ticket for group-stage games 60 days out was $1,040.52 on FIFA's resale market. In Dallas, where global icon [Lionel Messi](http://espn.com/soccer/player/_/id/45843/lionel-messi) will play two matches, the average cheapest ticket for group-stage games was $1,028.\n\n[U.S. President Donald Trump also chimed in](https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/48885837/new-jersey-new-york-subpoena-20926-fifa-world-cup-tickets), saying, \"I wouldn't pay it either, to be honest with you,\" when discussing World Cup tickets that could go for more than $1,000.\n\nAs for Marshall, the fan from California who is going to 11 games this summer, he's spending more than $10,000 on tickets alone.\n\nSpeaking of finals, it's worth comparing the World Cup to the May 30 [UEFA Champions League](https://www.espn.com/soccer/league/_/name/UEFA.CHAMPIONS) final, which is widely recognized as the pinnacle of club soccer. [Prices were set between $80 and $1,090](https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/48223786/champions-league-final-tickets-frozen-last-year-prices) for the event, and unlike FIFA's World Cup selling policy, tickets had to be kept at face value on the resale market and had no fee charged to the seller.\n\nCompared to the average cheapest ticket price of $446.89 for group-stage games as of June 1, L.A. residents could instead spend as little as $28 on an Olympic 2028 ticket. In Miami, the cheapest ticket for group-stage games seen on June 1 costs an average of $960.54, which is more than the cheapest race-day ticket of $799 for [Formula 1](https://www.espn.com/f1/)'s Miami Grand Prix, an event that has become notorious for its extravagance and exclusivity.\n\nCompared with prices 60 days out from the World Cup, the average cheapest ticket price across all 11 U.S. host cities fell by an average of 37%.\n\n\"They're either going to land at thousands of dollars of tickets or they're going to end up coming down. I know right now ... there are plenty of tickets that are available around the country for less than $200 for the World Cup.\n\n\"They will depend on the eventual supply and demand in the market, but if I had to guess, I would expect a bifurcated market: Some tickets will fall as the event approaches, while the most scarce and culturally significant matches may remain extremely expensive.\"\n\nThat'll cost you between $151 and $300 for FIFA-approved options.\n\nMeanwhile, [NJ Transit will charge $98 to get to MetLife Stadium](https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/48757359/price-world-cup-bus-new-york-reduced-fan-outcry), and in Boston, [an express bus will cost $95](https://www.espn.com/
